14:## Definition and Classification
16:A 2025 international Delphi consensus (Krapf et al.) standardized terminology:<sup>[[1]](#ref1)</sup>
27:## Prevalence
37:## Etiology
43:| **Genitourinary syndrome of menopause (GSM)** | Hypoestrogenic vulvar changes may include clitoral-hood phimosis.<sup>[[3]](#ref3)</sup> |
51:## Clinical Presentation
61:## Diagnosis
70:## Treatment Ladder
74:### 1. Medical (first-line for the underlying dermatosis)
78:- **Topical testosterone is not a substitute.** In the Cochrane review of genital LS, four trials found no significant benefit from topical testosterone, dihydrotestosterone or progesterone, and testosterone used as maintenance after clobetasol worsened symptoms; a retrospective series found higher symptom-response rates at 6 months with clobetasol 0.05% than with testosterone 2% (91.7% versus 77.5%) and lower recurrence (6.7% versus 20%).<sup>[[17]](#ref17)[[19]](#ref19)</sup>
80:### 2. Office lysis of symptomatic adhesions
82:For selected symptomatic clitoral adhesions, after consent and adequate analgesia, office-based lysis with fine Jacobsen mosquito forceps to separate prepuce from glans, with removal of trapped smegma and keratin pearls. In the Myers 2022 retrospective series, 41 of 61 eligible patients responded (67%); the following are respondent-reported outcomes without a control group or validated pre-post instrument:<sup>[[4]](#ref4)</sup>
89:In a later pre-post study of women with partial clitoral phimosis and keratin pearls, 32 of 74 eligible patients responded after office lysis with pearl excision. Among respondents, mean clitoral pain fell from 6.9 to 2.5 on an 11-point scale and FSFI rose from 12.1 to 17.7; 28% reported recurrence, with a median of 2 repeat procedures.<sup>[[15]](#ref15)</sup> A 2026 meta-analysis of these interventions was retracted after an error in the Krapf sample size propagated through its analyses, so its pooled estimates should not be used.<sup>[[16]](#ref16)</sup> Counsel about pain and recurrence: among 41 Myers survey respondents, 27% recalled procedural pain, 54% painful recovery, 46% recurrent adhesions, and 24% repeat lysis.<sup>[[4]](#ref4)</sup>
93:### 3. Surgical management (persistent symptomatic phimosis)
103:In a 2026 cohort of mixed LS surgery for introital stenosis or clitoral phimosis, 86 of 148 identified patients responded and 71 had paired FSFI scores rising from 15.0 to 22.7. Among the 86 respondents, 80 (93%) were satisfied, 29 (33.7%) reported recurrent scarring, and 9 (10.5%) underwent revision a mean 3.9 years after initial surgery; the mean surgery-to-survey interval was 4.35 years. Clitoral-only outcomes were not reported. It had no control group.<sup>[[18]](#ref18)</sup>
115:#### Surgicel application after lysis
117:In a three-adolescent LS case report, oxidized regenerated cellulose (Surgicel) was sutured to the exposed clitoral-hood and labial surfaces after sharp lysis; none had recurrence at one year, without a comparator to show that the material prevented it.<sup>[[11]](#ref11)</sup>
121:## Postoperative Considerations
123:- **Reagglutination is a reported failure mode.** Mild reagglutination occurred in 5 of 20 women with LS after CO₂ laser release<sup>[[7]](#ref7)</sup>; recurrent adhesions were reported by 19 of 41 (46%) after office lysis in Myers<sup>[[4]](#ref4)</sup> and by 28% of 32 respondents after office lysis with keratin-pearl excision.<sup>[[15]](#ref15)</sup>
125:- **Counsel about perioperative harms.** In a mixed LS-surgery cohort, reported events included anesthesia or positioning problems, urinary symptoms or infection, and bleeding requiring a suture.<sup>[[18]](#ref18)</sup>
130:## Key Principles
132:- Clitoral phimosis is distinct from clitoral adhesions; the Krapf 2025 Delphi consensus standardized this distinction.<sup>[[1]](#ref1)</sup>
136:- **Reagglutination** occurs after laser release and office lysis, with reported recurrence varying across cohorts and procedures. Continue disease-directed anti-inflammatory treatment when LS or LP is present.<sup>[[7]](#ref7)[[4]](#ref4)[[15]](#ref15)</sup>
