import VideoCards from '@site/src/components/VideoCards';

# The Penis

The **penis** has paired corpora cavernosa whose tunica, arterial inflow, veno-occlusive outflow control, and cavernous nerves support erection. The corpus spongiosum carries the urethra for urine and semen passage, while the dorsal nerves provide somatic sensation.<sup>[[1]](#ref1)[[2]](#ref2)[[3]](#ref3)[[17]](#ref17)</sup>

---

## Gross Anatomy and Fascial Layers

The penis is composed of three erectile bodies: the paired corpora cavernosa (the rigidity engines) and the single midline corpus spongiosum (which surrounds the urethra and expands distally into the glans). Proximal attachments include the crura of the corpora cavernosa to the ischiopubic rami, the bulb of the corpus spongiosum to the perineal membrane, and the ischiocavernosus and bulbospongiosus muscles over each.<sup>[[3]](#ref3)</sup> Surface landmarks at the glans matter for exam and reconstruction: the corona overhangs the shaft, the ventral surface forms a "V" with the frenulum attaching just proximal to its point, and the meatus sits ventrally at the tip of the glans with a slit-like appearance.<sup>[[3]](#ref3)</sup>

![Transverse mid-shaft penile cross-section showing the corpora, spongiosum, tunica, Buck's fascia, and dorsal neurovascular bundle](/img/diagrams/penile-cross-section.svg)

*Transverse mid-shaft section (dorsal up). The paired **corpora cavernosa**, each wrapped in **tunica albuginea** and carrying a central **deep cavernosal artery**, meet at the **intercavernosal septum**; the ventral **corpus spongiosum** carries the **urethra**. The **deep dorsal vein** lies near 12 o'clock, while the paired **dorsal arteries** and **dorsal nerves** course laterally beneath **Buck's fascia**<sup>[[3]](#ref3)</sup> — the relationships that define the dissection planes for Peyronie's plaque surgery, prosthesis placement, and nerve-sparing. (Original WARWIKI schematic)*

### External layers (superficial to deep)

| Layer | Composition | Operative relevance |
|---|---|---|
| **Skin** | Thin and mobile; shaft skin is usually hairless, but ventral skin near the raphe may carry hair<sup>[[47]](#ref47)</sup> | The mobile skin tube is the plane of degloving for circumcision, hypospadias repair, Peyronie's curvature repair, and skin-substitution urethroplasty |
| **Dartos fascia** | Loose subcutaneous tissue with smooth-muscle fibers and **axial-pattern vessels from the external pudendal artery**<sup>[[3]](#ref3)[[4]](#ref4)</sup> | The vascular plane for axial and island penile skin flaps (Orandi, Quartey, McAninch) in substitution urethroplasty; Blandy's urethroplasty uses a scrotal flap.<sup>[[42]](#ref42)</sup> |
| **Buck's fascia** | Dense fibroelastic envelope encircling all three erectile bodies; splits into two leaves ventrally around the corpus spongiosum<sup>[[3]](#ref3)[[4]](#ref4)[[5]](#ref5)</sup> | Carries the **dorsal neurovascular bundle** (dorsal artery, dorsal nerves, deep dorsal vein). Dissection under Buck's is the Peyronie's / curvature plane; dissection above Buck's is the circumcision / degloving plane |
| **Tunica albuginea** | Bilayered collagen jacket around each corpus cavernosum<sup>[[6]](#ref6)[[7]](#ref7)</sup> | The rigidity-bearing structure; the plane of corporotomy for penile prosthesis and of plication/incision for Peyronie's correction |

### The tunica albuginea — the key structural layer

The tunica is bilayered:<sup>[[6]](#ref6)[[7]](#ref7)</sup>

- **Inner circular layer.** A complete 360° lamina with radiating intracavernous pillars (struts) that augment the septum and confer internal structural support.
- **Outer longitudinal layer.** Incomplete, approximately 300° coat; absent ventrally between the 5- and 7-o'clock positions where the corpus spongiosum and urethra lie.<sup>[[7]](#ref7)</sup> A Peyronie's plaque reduces local tunical extensibility and can bend the erect penis toward the involved side.<sup>[[24]](#ref24)</sup>

**Mechanical properties.** The tunica is collagen-dominant with elastic fibers and is anisotropic. In porcine tunica, type I collagen made up 95.5% of total protein, and Young's modulus was 60 MPa longitudinally vs 8 MPa circumferentially (six pigs); human values have not been reported in the same way.<sup>[[8]](#ref8)</sup> The tunica has regional thickness differences in the cited developmental-anatomy work.<sup>[[9]](#ref9)</sup> Plication shortens the convex side to balance the shorter plaque side; selection depends on erectile function, length and deformity rather than a presumed universal plaque direction.<sup>[[24]](#ref24)</sup>

**Why dissection planes matter.** Three parallel dissection planes drive most open penile surgery:
1. **Above Buck's (subcutaneous / dartos).** Degloving for circumcision, phalloplasty skin mobilization, hypospadias.
2. **Under Buck's, on the tunica.** Peyronie's plication / incision, curvature correction, venous ligation.
3. **Into the tunica (corporotomy).** Penile prosthesis insertion, evacuation of ischemic priapism, Peyronie's plaque excision with graft.
Misidentifying these planes can injure the dorsal neurovascular structures or lead to implant malposition.

### Ligamentous support

The penis is suspended by two ligaments relevant to reconstructive surgery:

- **Fundiform ligament.** Superficial, from the linea alba to the penile dartos.
- **Suspensory ligament.** Yiee and Baskin describe a suspensory-ligament extension of Buck's fascia attaching the penis to the pubis; in an eight-cadaver dissection, Danino et al. instead distinguished a loose suspensory band from the deeper dense vertical and arcuate attachments between pubis and tunica. Partial release of the fundiform and suspensory bands was described without destabilization in that cadaveric model.<sup>[[3]](#ref3)[[35]](#ref35)</sup>

---

## Erectile Tissue and Corporal Architecture

The corpora cavernosa contain a three-dimensional network of endothelium-lined sinusoidal spaces separated by bundles of trabecular smooth muscle that insert at multiple points into the fibrous skeleton.<sup>[[3]](#ref3)[[10]](#ref10)</sup> The two cavernosa communicate through the midline pectiniform septum, which is fenestrated in the pendulous penis and complete proximally near the crura.<sup>[[6]](#ref6)</sup> The fenestrated pendulous septum permits communication between the two corpora.<sup>[[6]](#ref6)</sup> During prosthesis insertion, crossover into the opposite corpus is a complication to recognize and correct.<sup>[[33]](#ref33)</sup> The corpus spongiosum has the same sinusoidal architecture but thinner walls and larger sinuses, so it remains compressible during erection and does not occlude the urethra.

The subtunical venular plexus lies between the sinusoids and the tunica albuginea and is the mechanical substrate of veno-occlusion.<sup>[[12]](#ref12)</sup>

