 ## Treatment Ladder
@@ -75,15 +79,21 @@
 
 - **High-potency topical corticosteroids.** An individualized induction and maintenance regimen (see the linked LS page). Prevents progression but does not reverse established architectural changes.<sup>[[3]](#ref3)[[6]](#ref6)[[12]](#ref12)</sup>
 - **Maintenance for LS or LP.** Continue individualized anti-inflammatory treatment after release when an inflammatory dermatosis is present; routine steroid use solely to prevent readhesion without LS or LP has not been established.<sup>[[7]](#ref7)[[3]](#ref3)[[4]](#ref4)</sup>
+- **LS regimen.** The BASHH 2024 vulval guideline gives clobetasol ointment once daily for four weeks, on alternate days for four weeks, then twice weekly for four weeks, followed by maintenance adjusted to examination and symptoms.<sup>[[23]](#ref23)</sup> In a 507-patient vulvar-LS cohort, adherent versus partially adherent patients had symptom suppression in 93.3% versus 58.0% and new adhesions/scarring in 3.4% versus 40.0% during follow-up.<sup>[[25]](#ref25)</sup>
+- **Hypoestrogenic tissue.** When genitourinary syndrome of menopause contributes to thin, dry vulvar tissue, address the estrogen deficiency separately from any fixed adhesion. Topical estrogen is used after lysis of postmenopausal labial fusion; in a 14-patient series, estrogen-only maintenance was followed by recurrence in 3 of 3 patients, while a later estrogen-plus-dilation protocol had no recurrence over a mean 24 months. Those observations concern labial fusion and vaginal patency rather than isolated clitoral adhesions.<sup>[[27]](#ref27)</sup>
 - **Topical testosterone is not a substitute.** In the Cochrane review of genital LS, four trials found no significant benefit from topical testosterone, dihydrotestosterone or progesterone, and testosterone used as maintenance after clobetasol worsened symptoms; a retrospective series found higher symptom-response rates at 6 months with clobetasol 0.05% than with testosterone 2% (91.7% versus 77.5%) and lower recurrence (6.7% versus 20%).<sup>[[17]](#ref17)[[19]](#ref19)</sup>
 
 ### 2. Office lysis of symptomatic adhesions
 
-For selected symptomatic clitoral adhesions, after consent and adequate analgesia, office-based lysis with fine Jacobsen mosquito forceps to separate prepuce from glans, with removal of trapped smegma and keratin pearls. In the Myers 2022 retrospective series, 41 of 61 eligible patients responded (67%); the following are respondent-reported outcomes without a control group or validated pre-post instrument:<sup>[[4]](#ref4)</sup>
+Select patients with a visible or palpable adhesion, trapped debris or pearl, and a symptom plausibly localized to that finding. Document the amount of exposed glans, tenderness, and coexisting LS or hypoestrogenic change. After consent, topical anesthetic and/or a dorsal clitoral nerve block can provide local anesthesia; assess effect before manipulation. Fine Jacobsen mosquito forceps separate the prepuce from the glans in the adhesion plane. Remove exposed smegma or keratin pearls; the endpoint is full visualization of the glans and corona without deep dissection into the body or dorsal bundle.<sup>[[4]](#ref4)[[24]](#ref24)</sup>
 
+In the Myers 2022 retrospective series, 41 of 61 eligible patients responded (67%); the following are respondent-reported outcomes without a control group or validated pre-post instrument:<sup>[[4]](#ref4)</sup>
+
 - **76% (19/25 pain-item respondents)** reported pain improvement <sup>[[4]](#ref4)</sup>
 - **63% (25/40)** reported improved arousal <sup>[[4]](#ref4)</sup>
 - **64% (25/39)** reported improved orgasmic function <sup>[[4]](#ref4)</sup>
+- **38% (6/16)** who had been unable to reach orgasm with external clitoral stimulation reported doing so afterward <sup>[[4]](#ref4)</sup>
+- **83% (34/41)** were satisfied with their decision to undergo lysis <sup>[[4]](#ref4)</sup>
 - **93% of survey respondents** would recommend the procedure <sup>[[4]](#ref4)</sup>
 
