 - **Biopsy** any new hyperkeratotic lesion, erosion, ulceration, or suspicious mass in a patient with LS; biopsy may also clarify an uncertain diagnosis or treatment failure. Continue vulvar surveillance for LS-associated SCC.<sup>[[22]](#ref22)[[6]](#ref6)</sup>
 - Examine the clitoris in patients with sexual dysfunction, vulvar symptoms, or known vulvar dermatoses. In patients with clitorodynia, look for adhesions, debris, or keratin pearls and assess pelvic-floor muscle tone and possible neuropathic causes of pain.<sup>[[4]](#ref4)[[24]](#ref24)</sup>
 
+A **keratin pearl** is a firm focus of keratin trapped between the prepuce and glans; it can be painful even when the prepuce is partly retractable. Distinguish it from a separate enlarging cyst or suspicious vulvar lesion before offering lysis.<sup>[[15]](#ref15)[[24]](#ref24)</sup>
